{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means fewer distractions—not fewer comforts.** In a small flat, each piece should serve a purpose and work well with the others.\n\n- **A restrained palette:** a few related colours, with wood or textured fabrics for warmth.\n- **Simple furniture:** clean shapes, sized for the room—not lots of tiny pieces.\n- **Useful storage:** closed cupboards keep everyday clutter out of sight.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so moving between doors and windows feels easy.\n- **Complete, comfortable spaces:** you still need seating, a reachable table, lighting and somewhere to put your things.\n\nFor your combined living–dining room, that could mean two clearly defined areas with coordinated furniture and an open route between them. Your existing warm off-white walls could be a good starting point.\n\nThe goal is **calm and practical, not empty or all-white**.", "conversationId": "a8378ab4b87d4734bb70870d9d4f3f09"}
